ion  has tested negative for covid  right then so what that means is you're  not going to be having someone do a pcr  test wait a few days to get the result  and perhaps unknowingly spread  the virus within their own team or to an  opponent who may be living somewhere  else in the country  so cutting down on that and the contact  tracing required  is a huge deal and that's why people  refer to these daily tests as  as game changers so you have that part  of the puzzle as well and then there's  also  very specific detailed protocols a  player that tests positive is not  available to return competition for 21  days which is longer than  the other leagues that are playing right  now because they're going to go through  a series of cardiac screening  um to address concerns related to  myocarditis and other heart  inflammations  so you have a couple of different things  happening here as well  as restrictions about okay if you're at  a certain positivity rate  on your roster or in your community  you're not  able to play and it would trigger a  shutdown so there's very specific  thresholds  um from a health and safety standpoint  that these teams are going to have to  meet on a weekly basis  in order to play games so it's very very  detailed and it answers a lot of those  questions that the presidents had  four weeks ago and like you said some  people were very angry with the big  ten's decision to not play  do you think they should have waited it  out like the other conferences  do you think now that we have these new  things in place  that it was kind of a bad call on their  part or do you think they were really  just trying to do what they thought was  best for athletes  i mean i think they thought that they  were doing what's best for their  athletes and these are university  presidents  who have to make decisions about the  entire campus right like they're dealing  with stuff beyond just sports  and they don't want to have something  that's going to put the entire c